2026-11-21 19:05:05,899 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-1: this is what i remember about my grandfather doubleday's sawmill in north dana
2026-11-21 19:05:05,899 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-10: as i recall it the dam was about ten feet high
2026-11-21 19:05:05,899 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-11: and it was built of stone
2026-11-21 19:05:05,899 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-12: at one end of the dam was the control works
2026-11-21 19:05:05,899 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-13: and from this a wooden pinstock ran down to the mill
2026-11-21 19:05:05,899 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-14: this pinstock as i recall was square made of four large planks each about two feet wide making the pinstock about two feet square
2026-11-21 19:05:05,899 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-15: and held together by iron straps and iron bars
2026-11-21 19:05:05,899 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-16: this pinstock led down to the ladder wheel underneath the mill
2026-11-21 19:05:05,899 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-17: this was not the older type of overshot wheel or undershot wheel but it was a turbine type which probably at that time was quite modern
2026-11-21 19:05:05,899 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-18: this turbine was inside of a steel case which i remember as being about four feet in diameter and perhaps four feet tall
2026-11-21 19:05:05,899 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-19: the pinstock could be opened and closed by a wheel up on the floor of the mill
2026-11-21 19:05:05,899 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-2: it was part of the place which he bought in 1883 the year my father myron doubleday
2026-11-21 19:05:05,899 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-20: and the ladder turbine or ladder wheel drove the main shaft which in turn drove a leather belt which i remember as being over a foot wide perhaps fifteen inches wide
2026-11-21 19:05:05,899 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-21: and this led up to the shaft of the saw
2026-11-21 19:05:05,899 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-22: of course it also ran any other machinery that was in the mill too
2026-11-21 19:05:05,899 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-23: the saw was probably about four feet in diameter so it was capable of handling rods up to two feet in diameter
2026-11-21 19:05:05,899 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-24: the mill was normally operated by three men a roller a sawyer and a marker
2026-11-21 19:05:05,899 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-25: and if the fourth was available why he handled the slabs
2026-11-21 19:05:05,899 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-26: the roller took the rods which were on the skidway and rolled them from there onto the carriage
2026-11-21 19:05:05,899 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-27: now the carriage ran on a set of rails similar to railroad rails only smaller
2026-11-21 19:05:05,899 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-28: and the carriage was probably around eighteen or twenty feet long altogether and was made so that the upper part of the carriage could be moved crossways of the carriage toward the saw
2026-11-21 19:05:05,899 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-29: the carriage ran back and forth right past the saw
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-3: was born
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-30: so the roller rolled a log onto the carriage and it was held there by two heavy iron dogs which were sharp pointed and raised up and down on steel posts
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-31: the roller would jam these two dogs into the log and this would hold it
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-32: the sawyer would then move the carriage over by means of a handle which he could pull on move the carriage over so that as it passed by the saw the saw would cut off a section of the log
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-33: and the first section that was cut off was called the slab because this contained mostly bark
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-34: and usually the way they did it was they would saw the slab in other words take the first cut
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-35: then the sawyer would run the carriage backwards and then move the carriage over a certain amount
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-36: if he wanted a one inch board he would move it one inch if he wanted a two inch board he would move it two inches and so on
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-37: and then he would run the carriage forward pass the saw again and the saw would cut off whatever thickness of board the sawyer had moved the carriage for
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-38: after they'd taken off several boards this way they would then roll the log over so that the part which had already been sawed would be flat up against the carriage
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-39: and this time the two dogs were turned around so that a shorter dog would be holding the log in place on the carriage so that the saw could saw up fairly close to the end
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-4: and when my grandfather myron doubleday bought it it was known as the gleason place
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-40: then they would continue the process of running the carriage forward past the saw the first cut would take off a slab and then the next cut would take off boards which had round edges in other words they still had the bark on the two sides of them
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-41: now if they wanted to have square ed boards they would merely cut four slabs off of the log leaving a four sided log which had no bark left on it at all
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-42: and they would then proceed as before and this would result in square ed boards
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-43: now as the carriage went past the saw the board which had been sawed off from the log would just fall over onto a bench
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-44: and then the marker would mark on it the number of board feet in that board of course a board foot is a board one foot wide one foot long and one inch thick
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-45: so if you had a board which was ten feet long one foot wide and one inch thick that board would have ten board feet in it
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-46: and the marker would add a piece of heavy crayon and he would mark a ten on that board
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-47: if the board was the same size but two inches thick it would have twenty board feet in it and he'd mark a twenty on it and so on
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-48: he also had a peg board hanging on the wall and by moving the pegs into different holes in this board he was able to keep track of the number of board feet of lumber that was sawed during the day by the mill
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-49: and on a good day they would saw several thousand board feet of lumber
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-5: and it included not only the farm and pastures but the mill site as well
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-50: a keenster would then take this lumber which had just been sawed and load it onto his wagon or which was in the wintertime his sled
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-51: and with a pair of horses he would draw it from the mill out to the area where it was stuck up so to speak
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-52: sticking up this lumber consisted of first laying down some heavy pieces of slab on the ground so as to get a fairly level foundation to start with and also get it up off of the ground because if the lumber was piled directly on the ground it would rot very quickly
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-53: he would lay a couple of these heavy pieces of slab crossways and then start laying the planks on top of them and he would scrap with a pile perhaps five or six feet wide
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-54: when he got one layer of planks or boards laid down he would then lay a couple of board sticks crossways of this layer of boards and then put another layer on top
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-55: these board sticks were made by taking slabs in the mill and sawing them first into the top of length five six feet whatever size they wanted for the lumber piles and then splitting the slabs by means of a saw into pieces which would then be five feet long and about one inch thick
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-56: they would vary in width but this didn't make any difference the core
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-57: and these board sticks were what separated the layers of boards or planks in the piles of lumber
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-58: and this allowed air to circulate through the piles of lumber and therefore cure or dry the wood in the lumber
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-59: and it was ordinarily cured for about a year before it was sold
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-6: the mill was a water powered mill located on fever brook in north dana
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-60: then if it was sold it might have been sold to the local box factory the swiss triller box company in north dana for making wooden boxes in which case it would be drawn by a team of horses from the yard so to speak where the piles of lumber were down to the box shop
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-61: or if it was sold to some outer town concern my grandfather and my father sold quite a lot of lumber to the furniture factories over around gardner and templeton
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-62: if it was sold to one of them it would be drawn down to north dana and loaded into railroad box cars and then shipped by railroad over to the furniture factory
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-63: by the time i was big enough to see the mill my grandfather had stopped operating it and instead it was operated by frank doubleday
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-64: but i used to go over there as a kid quite often because it was only a quarter of a mile or so from my home there in north dana
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-65: and particularly one period when my uncle glenn page worked there for frank doubleday and glenn was the roller
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-66: so i used to go over and watch him and he would instruct me in the proper techniques of rolling and so on and so forth and i would also watch the operation of the mill
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-67: another thing too i used to go over there quite often in the springtime spearing fish
2026-11-21 19:05:05,900 - kalpy.graphs - 134092083865280 - 1 - DEBUG - 1-68: because after the mill had operated all day and then they shut down around five o'clock in the afternoon
